<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chattuarii</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Chattuarii were a Germanic people of the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, mentioned by Roman writers from the 1st century CE. They are counted among the older peoples who merged into the Frankish confederation from the 3rd century, and a district on the lower Rhine, the Hattuarian gau, preserved their name in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Chattuarii, also spelled Attuarii, lived east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> delta and north of the Lippe, outside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> but close to it, near what is now the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>. Their name, reconstructed by Günter Neumann as *Chattwarijiz, is generally read as inhabitants of the Chatti lands, formed like the names of the Ampsivarii, the dwellers on the Ems, or the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavarians">Baiuvarii</a> and Boructuarii, who lived where the Boii and Bructeri once had.</p><p>Wagner and Rübekeil note that the name is always spelled with -tt-, unlike that of the Chatti, and propose that the Chattuarii were newcomers who spoke a Germanic language, probably Suebi. Petrikovits instead saw the name as evidence that the Chatti had themselves once lived further north.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/velleius-paterculus">Velleius Paterculus</a>, the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> in 4 CE and attacked them after a people of the modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and before the Bructeri. Strabo, writing about 23 CE, counts them among the tribes allied under the Cherusci and made poor after their defeat by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanicus">Germanicus</a>, and they appear among the peoples shown at his triumph in 17 CE. The Laterculus Veronensis records that in the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallienus">Gallienus</a>, from 260 to 268, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> lost five communities beyond the Rhine, among them the Gallovari, generally believed to be the Chattuarii.</p><p>In 360 they appear again as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> living near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>. Ammianus Marcellinus tells how the emperor Julian crossed the river from Xanten into the district of the Attuarii, a turbulent people then plundering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, and surprised them where they felt safe behind their rugged roads.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> their name passed to two early medieval gaus on either side of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ripuarian-franks">Ripuarian Franks</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>: one near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruhr">Ruhr</a>, the other near the Niers between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Maas</a> and the Rhine, named in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-meerssen">Treaty of Meerssen</a> of 870. Some were settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> as laeti, giving their name to the pagus attuariorum south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/langres">Langres</a>. They may be the Hetwaras of the Old English poem <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beowulf">Beowulf</a>, allied with the Frisians against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geats">Geats</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hygelac">Hygelac</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chattuarii” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Franks emerged on the lower Rhine in the middle of the 3rd century, not as a migrating people but as a new grouping of older peoples who had long lived beside the Roman frontier, among them the Chamavi, <b>Chattuarii</b>, Bructeri, Amsivarii and Tencteri, and perhaps the Sugambri.</blockquote><a href="/empire/franks">Franks · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
